WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:06.160
[Sarah Richards:] Walter's going to narrate the film as we play it.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.120
[Walter:] The first pictures start out at the power plant.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:15.500
It's some of the buildings connected to the laundry building.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:27.700
This is an aerial view from the top of the mountain of the whole camp.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:42.600
This is another view taken, an aerial view, it's some of the Dixon cottages.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:59.120
Cottages and the chapel in the background... these were designed by Dr. Dixon.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.620
[Sarah Richards:] Who was Dr. Dixon?

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:14.060
[Walter:] He was, Dr. Dixon was the first Director of Health, Pennsylvania.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:35.000
This is more aerial view of the surrounding mountains, shows the background of this whole area.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:44.520
This section is the golf course, which was built by the Health Department.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.680
[Sarah Richards:] Was the golf course for patients?

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:56.820
[Walter:] Staff. The golf course was for staff and employees.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:08.100
This is still more of an aerial view.

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:24.580
This is a closer picture of the golf course area.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:38.440
These pictures were taken from Snowy Mountain Fire Tower, which would be south of the sanatorium.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:54.960
That picture was of the cemetery.

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:58.960
[Sarah Richards:] Is this where patients were buried?

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:00.780
[Walter:] Patients were buried here.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:05.740
This is the Children's Hospital.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:15.720
It was originally for patients that were too sick to be out taking care of themselves.

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:21.540
Then, during World War I up until 1923,

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:28.280
it was filled by veterans of World War I that had been gassed in the service.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:34.080
Then in 1924, why the children were moved in.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:35.780
[Sarah Richards:] What is this building?

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:38.480
That building was the infirmary.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:43.320
These are open-air pavilions on the main hospital grounds.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:50.300
Beside all these cottages, there was 56 of these type of cottages,

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:54.760
they decided to make most of these cottages with open-air pavilions

00:03:54.760 --> 00:04:00.540
with chairs that the patients sat in for hours and hours.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.580
[Sarah Richards:] Were the cottages heated?

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:09.280
[Walter:] The cottages had steam heat in the center aisle of the cottage in the hallway.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:12.980
There wasn't any heat in the rooms unless you had the door open.

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:16.480
This is nurses from the nurses' training.

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:21.160
It ran from 1924 to 1935.

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:32.700
They had a two-year training course for nurses to be trained for TB patients.

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:48.460
This is the store that's on the main grounds, where supplies could be,

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:54.900
newspapers, anything along that line, if they need stationery,

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:57.600
anything along that line could be bought at this store.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:04:59.720
[Sarah Richards:] For the patients?

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:00.840
[Walter:] For the patients.

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:03.370
This is the chapel built in 1911.

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:09.180
[Coughing]

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:19.940
This was the original, one of the first nurses' home buildings.

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:24.220
Later on it was used as office buildings.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:29.440
This was the first large dining room.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:39.000
These are all patients that are bathing in the sun, fresh air, and sunlight.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:42.380
[Sarah Richards:] Were male and females segregated?

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:46.960
[Walter:] Yes, there was what they call an upper and lower men's camp,

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:49.600
and an upper and lower women's camp.

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:56.520
There were different sections ...

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:58.420
[Sarah Richards:] What is that man doing there?

00:05:58.420 --> 00:06:08.680
[Walter:] He is probably a, uh, either a doctor or an aide that's just supervising,

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:16.440
makes sure that everybody is getting sun, and keeping order. [laughs]

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:27.820
They examined the patients regular.

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:35.640
They would be out and ... and doctors would examine them to make sure

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:43.800
that everything that was possible at that time could be done for them.

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:12.460
These patients are holding mirrors with the sunlight at their back shining in the mirror,

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:20.140
and the light goes into their mouth so it's a way that they felt would cure their throats...

00:07:20.140 --> 00:07:22.780
heal up their throats with the sunlight.

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:33.820
This was the assembly building, had been used for a theater.

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:43.840
It was the theater, and also there was a school, a business training school and that building had burnt.

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:57.860
And about two years later there was a new building, um, to replace that one.

00:07:59.980 --> 00:08:07.940
And there was a lot of deer in the area, and some of them would get tame, come right in around the cottages.

00:08:14.140 --> 00:08:21.580
This would be at the Mont Alto train station, would be about five miles from here, at the foot of the mountain.

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:26.660
These are children that they'd bring in here in the summer,

00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:30.740
mostly from the cities, they were called Fresh Air children.

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:36.760
And they would stay in a building back of the Children's Hospital,

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:42.580
it was a long building back of the Children's Hospital, screened on the sides,

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:45.560
and they would stay there for the summer months.

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:58.120
And they just, ah, they felt that the fresh air, and the good food would be a help for them.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:08.860
Everybody was brought up in trucks from the train station to the top of the mountain.

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:18.280
Then the children were all examined when they come in,

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:30.500
checked for any diseases, so that they wouldn't start a epidemic for the whole area.

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:32.380
Everybody was tested.

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:36.920
They received a few shots which they didn't care for.

00:09:54.580 --> 00:10:04.120
All of these tests were made before these children were put in with any of the other, any of the other children.

00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:13.020
Everybody received a complete haircut.

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:24.040
[Sarah Richards:] What was they reason for the haircuts?

00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:35.680
[Walter:] The haircut was because some of the children had head lice when they came, or other skin diseases,

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:39.200
and they could check for this and treat it immediately and.....

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:41.700
This was in the bath houses.

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:49.420
Everybody had their own toothbrush that they kept with them,

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:53.640
and there was a, there were containers that had tooth powder,

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:57.580
you just went to the container and put powder on your brush and...

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:02.380
[Sarah Richards:] There was one container for everyone?

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:11.240
[Walter:] There would be containers, there'd probably be a dozen of these containers in each bath house.

00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:17.700
You just held the brush underneath and you pushed a button and the powder come down onto the brush.

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:27.980
And here they are just examining the children, looking to see what condition they are in.

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:46.420
[Sarah Richards:] Were the clothes the children wear supplied by the center?

00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:55.460
[Walter:] All clothing except your shoes were, and socks were supplied by the state.

00:11:55.460 --> 00:11:57.880
All clothing were supplied ...

00:11:57.880 --> 00:12:09.660
you bought your own shoes and you ah, if you had any repair on your shoes why that uh,

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:15.480
you had to pay for the repair of your shoes and your own stockings.

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:18.660
Everything else was supplied by the state.

00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:24.640
You paid for your own haircuts and your shoe repair.

00:12:31.900 --> 00:12:37.140
Some of these children were very thin when they came.

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:44.960
But with the food and the fresh air here, it did have a way of fattening you up pretty quick.

00:12:54.180 --> 00:13:03.520
This is the, it was twice a day, this was milk in the cans.

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:06.260
One of the men from the dining room would bring out the milk.

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:14.040
You went through, you picked up your cup, filled it, and then there was a container you put the cups in,

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:16.060
and they were taken back, and they would be washed,

00:13:16.060 --> 00:13:21.860
and they would be ready for the next time the milk cart came around.

00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:38.240
Milk was bought by the state from local farmers in this area.

00:13:38.500 --> 00:13:49.540
They had their own pasturizing plant there, and of course, all the milk was in refrigeration, and it was cool.

00:13:53.420 --> 00:14:00.900
These are the children sunbathing and a cloth over their faces

00:14:00.900 --> 00:14:05.660
was just to keep the sun out of their face and their eyes.

00:14:05.660 --> 00:14:10.960
They would lay for so long one way and then they would turn.

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:17.660
You gradually got a suntan without getting sunburnt.

00:14:17.900 --> 00:14:19.480
Nurses, doctors would watch...

00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:24.440
some people burned much easier than others,

00:14:24.450 --> 00:14:29.030
some could only be out a short time.

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:45.420
There was a lot of swings, see-saws, sliding boards, and  this building was located on a hill,

00:14:45.440 --> 00:14:53.100
and in the wintertime, there were probably two hundred sleds there for sliding.

00:14:54.640 --> 00:15:04.360
But you were out everyday unless you were real sick, real bad cold or something, you were out every day.

00:15:10.420 --> 00:15:25.260
This is the room, in one of the rooms, and everybody makes their own bed, and they are ready for inspection.

00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:30.980
The nurses and the doctors come through, check everyone.

00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:36.540
If anyones feels bad that day, this is when you tell them and you're taken into the clinic.

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:45.380
They check the hands, the fingernails, they make sure you've washed, make sure

00:15:45.380 --> 00:15:56.060
you have your hands clean, they check your beds to make sure the bed is made properly.

00:16:07.860 --> 00:16:13.020
There's probably 20 beds to a ward.

00:16:33.240 --> 00:16:41.240
Did you notice, the children all saluted when the doctor and the nurse left and when they enter?

00:16:53.200 --> 00:17:00.660
In this building, the boys were all on the second floor, and the girls were on the first floor.

00:17:01.860 --> 00:17:06.140
And at the north end of this building was an auditorium

00:17:08.860 --> 00:17:16.120
which was used for a chapel, for a theater, anything of that type.

00:17:16.340 --> 00:17:20.000
On the second floor was classrooms.

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:23.130
These children were all still in school.

00:17:24.260 --> 00:17:30.740
You went to school a half-day four days, or five days a week, a half-day at a time.

00:17:32.160 --> 00:17:37.200
You had practically the same classes you would have had at schools at home.

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:13.080
These are more tests that's given regular to make sure that everyone is okay.

00:18:53.260 --> 00:18:58.900
Besides all the swings and the playground equipment,

00:18:58.900 --> 00:19:10.380
there was Boy Scouts, Girl Scouts, there was movies and religious services every weekend.

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:33.740
One of the punishments if you didn't do everything that you were expected to do, or if you were a troublemaker,

00:19:33.760 --> 00:19:40.520
one of your punishments was you didn't get to go to the Saturday afternoon movie.

00:19:40.840 --> 00:19:44.300
You had to stay in bed while the movie was on.

00:19:46.620 --> 00:19:53.580
That seemed to be harder on most people than most any other punishment they received.

00:20:25.520 --> 00:20:30.060
Another thing for the girls was sewing classes.

00:20:31.060 --> 00:20:35.520
The were taught to make their own clothing for doll babies.

00:20:35.520 --> 00:20:44.160
There was a lot of organizations brought toys and doll babies

00:20:44.260 --> 00:20:48.400
and all kinds of toys at Christmas time that was given out to all.

00:20:48.400 --> 00:20:50.780
Everybody received a prize.

00:20:50.790 --> 00:20:57.970
There was a lot of games brought in by different organizations.

00:20:57.970 --> 00:21:07.690
Games that you had to play, marbles was another,

00:21:07.690 --> 00:21:16.630
a lot of marbles, everything along that line was furnished by different organizations.

00:21:54.500 --> 00:21:58.620
And this was every evening, everyone had their temperature taken,

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:07.460
And that was recorded.

00:22:10.280 --> 00:22:16.360
This was in the area of the sick, sicker wards;

00:22:16.360 --> 00:22:24.400
it wasn't so much so in the other part of the hospital.

00:22:34.120 --> 00:22:43.560
The hospital would hold 240 patients, and most of the time it was filled.

00:22:49.560 --> 00:22:52.740
It started in 19 ...

00:22:52.740 --> 00:22:58.200
the first children came in from Hamburg, Pennsylvania in 1924.

00:23:12.860 --> 00:23:18.280
That was mostly a way to settle arguments, you got a chance to put the boxing gloves on.

00:23:23.840 --> 00:23:29.660
But they made sure no one got hurt too bad.

00:23:44.600 --> 00:23:48.780
Even the girls liked the boxing gloves.

00:23:58.640 --> 00:24:04.580
This is more in the playground area.

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:15.920
[Sarah Richards:] And these are all still the Fresh Air kids,

00:24:15.920 --> 00:24:19.760
none of the real, none of the children who had tuberculosis?

00:24:19.960 --> 00:24:24.990
[Walter:] No, there isn't any tuberculosis children in this, in this area.

00:24:25.440 --> 00:24:34.680
Now, there was children in the main Children's Hospital that would be there for maybe a couple of years,

00:24:34.690 --> 00:24:37.920
two or three years 'til they got better,

00:24:37.920 --> 00:24:41.030
but what they called the Fresh Air children were only brought in through the summer months,

00:24:41.080 --> 00:24:44.100
they'd be here about three months, and then they went back.

00:24:44.380 --> 00:24:50.260
If some of them were real bad, then they would be kept in the main hospital,

00:24:51.200 --> 00:24:54.900
they would stay until they were able to go home.

00:25:00.900 --> 00:25:08.360
This hospital right here was closed in January 1940.

00:25:08.440 --> 00:25:13.800
At that time they were moved closer to the main hospital,

00:25:13.800 --> 00:25:20.300
into a new building which had been built in 1938-39.

00:25:20.300 --> 00:25:28.220
And later here's why this building was completely torn down.

00:25:28.220 --> 00:25:29.840
[Sarah Richards:] The pool was inside.

00:25:29.840 --> 00:25:36.800
[Walter:] Yes, these were inside pools.

00:25:36.800 --> 00:25:46.540
In 1936, there was another section built to this building for polio patients.

00:25:46.800 --> 00:25:56.980
And they had, ah, hot baths, massage tables, like that in there for the polio children.

00:25:57.740 --> 00:26:00.220
But from the...

00:26:00.440 --> 00:26:06.900
Children moved from this building to the new building in 1940.

00:26:07.160 --> 00:26:12.680
The polio ward was moved somewhere else.

00:26:12.680 --> 00:26:18.600
I don't know where it was moved to, but those children didn't come over to the new building.

00:26:26.800 --> 00:26:33.180
'Course everybody preferred the old building over the new.

00:26:35.700 --> 00:26:41.420
We were watched too closely in the new building. [laughs]

00:26:41.620 --> 00:26:45.800
We had more fun at the other place.

00:26:48.080 --> 00:26:50.440
[Sarah Richards:] So you were there during that transition?

00:26:50.440 --> 00:26:55.780
[Walter:] Yes, I was there. I moved into the new building.

00:26:57.380 --> 00:27:03.820
They did put, put weight on you with extra good meals and health and everything.

00:27:05.840 --> 00:27:19.460
When I came here I weighed 76 lbs, when I left I weighed 114.

00:27:29.460 --> 00:27:36.620
Now this is in the winter, the sliding area.

00:27:36.780 --> 00:27:40.400
You could slide for a long distance down that ...

00:27:40.410 --> 00:27:44.470
there were a few trees you had to watch, but there was a long distance you could slide,

00:27:44.470 --> 00:27:47.500
clear down, well it's all grown up now,

00:27:47.500 --> 00:27:52.480
but you could go clear down to the main road at the hospital.

00:27:57.720 --> 00:28:02.800
But regardless of the weather, you were out every day.

00:28:04.340 --> 00:28:07.480
You did bring your own topcoats.

00:28:07.580 --> 00:28:11.040
That was one thing, you furnished your own topcoat.

00:28:11.840 --> 00:28:14.540
There were many snowball battles.

00:28:19.140 --> 00:28:24.900
From the classrooms, which were above the auditorium, all these children were in school.

00:28:25.420 --> 00:28:30.340
And you went to school a half, four, five days a week, a half-day at a time.

00:28:32.560 --> 00:28:36.800
The classroom was above the auditorium,

00:28:36.810 --> 00:28:39.650
and it was so close to the mountain that you could look right out the window

00:28:39.650 --> 00:28:48.030
and see deer walking around, right out from the building.

00:29:47.020 --> 00:29:50.660
Again we are back to the milk line.

00:29:55.280 --> 00:29:58.920
That man that's running the milk line is a neighbor of mine right now.

00:29:58.920 --> 00:29:59.880
[Walter laughs.]

00:30:00.260 --> 00:30:04.740
He worked for the hospital at that time.

00:30:13.980 --> 00:30:17.040
And you were taken on, on hikes.

00:30:20.300 --> 00:30:21.540
You went camping,

00:30:21.760 --> 00:30:25.240
A lot of times, you would take a little bag of food.

00:30:25.250 --> 00:30:30.710
If you were gonna be out close to mealtime, then everybody had their own picnic lunch with them.

00:30:34.500 --> 00:30:36.580
You could walk the road without much trouble.

00:30:36.600 --> 00:30:45.060
At that time there wasn't traffic like there is today, and you just walked the main roads.

00:30:46.080 --> 00:30:50.060
[Sarah Richards:] And this was a coed activity, both boys and girls?

00:30:50.070 --> 00:30:57.710
[Walter:] Yeah, this would be yes, both boys and girls were doing this.

00:31:00.240 --> 00:31:03.820
You were taught how to make your own bed.

00:31:08.560 --> 00:31:14.400
There would be clean linen twice a week, or more if it was necessary.

00:31:16.080 --> 00:31:19.240
These were different nurses.

00:31:19.240 --> 00:31:25.700
There was a nurses' home built right to the south of the main, of this main Children's Hospital.

00:31:26.200 --> 00:31:30.580
They had their own nurses' home building right there.

00:31:30.580 --> 00:31:35.500
This hospital had its own power plant, its own kitchen, dining room.

00:31:52.440 --> 00:31:55.300
Even the nurses liked to use the swings.

00:32:02.160 --> 00:32:06.260
[Sarah Richards:] Did the nurses stay for a long time or was there a lot of turnover,

00:32:06.440 --> 00:32:10.120
where they would quit and new ones would come in, or become ill?

00:32:10.540 --> 00:32:12.680
[Walter:] A lot of nurses stayed.

00:32:13.080 --> 00:32:18.420
They would marry other employees there and live there,

00:32:18.420 --> 00:32:24.920
a lot of the nurses still, that came here as nurses, married somebody and still live here, in the area.

00:32:24.930 --> 00:32:29.910
There was a lot of patients that came here, when they were cured,

00:32:29.910 --> 00:32:36.850
they went to the nurses' training course at [?], on the grounds, and then they stayed here as nurses.

00:32:36.850 --> 00:32:42.430
Some went to other tuberculosis hospitals throughout the state to nurse.

00:32:42.430 --> 00:32:44.240
But a lot of them stayed here.

00:32:46.600 --> 00:32:49.260
And like I say, a lot of them still live here.

00:32:49.300 --> 00:32:53.860
They came from different parts of the state and they still live here, in the area.

00:33:05.000 --> 00:33:07.160
[Sarah Richards:] Were these girls or boys?

00:33:07.800 --> 00:33:12.320
[Walter:] That's probably, probably girls and boys both there.

00:33:12.320 --> 00:33:15.300
[Sarah Richards:] Girls would walk around without tops?

00:33:15.300 --> 00:33:20.440
[Walter:] No, the girls would have, would have had tops.

00:33:21.640 --> 00:33:31.760
This is one of the buses... they had at that time that would take children for rides.

00:33:32.220 --> 00:33:36.820
Some of these are leaving, been here for the summer.

00:33:36.820 --> 00:33:44.540
Some are leaving, going back to Mont Alto, which is five miles down the mountain from here.

00:33:44.680 --> 00:33:48.840
They get a train from there to Hagerstown,

00:33:48.840 --> 00:33:58.160
and then from Hagerstown they would take another train, to Philadelphia, Washington, Baltimore.

00:33:58.720 --> 00:34:04.180
Most would be, most that came in here would be from Pennsylvania,

00:34:04.180 --> 00:34:07.680
there would be some from other states, but most all would be from Pennsylvania.

00:34:20.160 --> 00:34:27.760
And when you went out on walks you lined up, sort of in military style.

00:34:29.130 --> 00:34:33.130
Everybody traveled together.

00:34:59.220 --> 00:35:10.020
Raising of the flag, every morning, evening.

00:35:10.030 --> 00:35:12.050
The flag went up, or the flag come down.

00:35:12.060 --> 00:35:24.460
You were out for the raising and the lowering of the flag,

00:35:24.700 --> 00:35:35.020
a lot of different little activities like that to keep your mind occupied, keep you out of other troubles.

00:35:43.920 --> 00:35:47.460
[Sarah Richards:] They all appear to be dressed up?

00:35:47.460 --> 00:35:51.020
[Walter:] These are children that are leaving.

00:35:51.020 --> 00:35:55.240
They've been here for the summer, and they're leaving and going back home.

00:36:07.380 --> 00:36:11.200
They're getting on it, boarding the train right now.

00:36:11.200 --> 00:36:18.740
